A revoke that recurses into each node before it clears it
takes a frame per level of the derivation tree,
and a deep enough tree overflows the kernel's stack.
It clears the same nodes and says what pays for its loop,
so the stack check catches it,
and the counting harness too, since the revoke it recurses into never asks intr_pending.
Where a pool below a node holds the node itself, the recursion destroys it first,
and the node the revoke then clears is memory the pool gave back.

Caught-by: stack-depth fuzz fuzz-pmp8 fuzz-rp2350 fuzz-smp2 fuzz-work
Report: the kernel reached RAM outside its objects
Report: the paid loop at kernel/cap.c took a second step without asking intr_pending

diff --git a/kernel/cap.c b/kernel/cap.c
--- a/kernel/cap.c
+++ b/kernel/cap.c
@@ -239,6 +239,7 @@ bool cap_revoke_below(struct cap *root, const struct cap *through, bool preempt)
                 return false;
             }
         } else {
+            cap_revoke_below(n, through, false);
             detach(n, true);
             clear_node(n);
         }
